ptions":["They must be nonzero.","They must have different dimensions.","Their sum is undefined.","They are equal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b2af211a7374d9d90fd13dcf","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch2_zero_vector_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must every vector space contain a zero vector?","options":["Because closure under addition alone forces it to exist.","Because $x+(-x)$ must give the scalar $0$ for every $x$.","Because an additive identity is one of the axioms.","Because every vector space is some $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with an origin."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"886d81f56095e99f4c6b6842","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch2_zero_vector_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What vector must be added to $(1,-4)$ to get the zero vector?","options":["$(0,0)$","$(-1,4)$","$(1,-4)$","$(-4,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dd623025bb9825c4fae5d05d","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch2_zero_vector_017_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which argument proves $0\\boldsymbol{v}=\\boldsymbol{0}$ for every vector $\\boldsymbol{v}$ in a vector space $V$?","options":["$0\\boldsymbol{v}=(0+0)\\boldsymbol{v}=0\\boldsymbol{v}+0\\boldsymbol{v}$; adding $-(0\\boldsymbol{v})$ to both sides gives $\\boldsymbol{0}=0\\boldsymbol{v}$.","It is one of the vector space axioms, so it needs no proof.","It is the definition of $\\boldsymbol{0}$: the zero vector is $0\\boldsymbol{v}$.","In $\\mathbb{R}^n$ every coordinate times $0$ is $0$, and this proves it in every vector space."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"99d164f818f828369ac12ab4","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_manual_batch2_zero_vector_019_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For vectors $\\boldsymbol{u},\\boldsymbol{v}$ in a vector space $V$ with $\\boldsymbol{u}\\neq\\boldsymbol{0}$, suppose $\\boldsymbol{u}+\\boldsymbol{v}=\\boldsymbol{u}$. What is $\\boldsymbol{v}$?","options":["Any vector of $V$","$-\\boldsymbol{u}$","$\\boldsymbol{0}$","$\\boldsymbol{u}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a19bfcf93660b1295abb624","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_manual_batch2_zero_vector_020_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Is the list $((1,2),(0,0))$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ linearly independent?","options":["No: $0(1,2)+1(0,0)=(0,0)$ is a combination with a nonzero coefficient.","Yes: $(0,0)$ is not a scalar multiple of $(1,2)$.","Yes: the zero vector adds no direction, so it can be ignored.","Yes: $(1,2)$ is nonzero, so the list is independent."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5732dfea0fdd42de1c3c4a85","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_zero_vector_086d0175_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which subset of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can be shown not to be a subspace just by checking whether it contains the zero vector $(0,0)$?","options":["$\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\colon y=2x+1\\}$","$\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\colon y=x^2\\}$","$\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\colon xy=0\\}$","$\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2\\colon y=2x\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f4031ee4ab9458ddca6b3504","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_zero_vector_086d0175_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $V$ be the real vector space of all functions $f\\colon\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$, with $(f+g)(x)=f(x)+g(x)$ and $(cf)(x)=cf(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Which element of $V$ is its zero vector?","options":["Any function $f$ with $f(0)=0$.","The constant function $u(x)=1$ for all $x$.","The constant function $z(x)=0$ for all $x$.","The real number $0$ itself."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3ed3daf868d3cfd0dfc26131","time_limit":40}]}}